# `yo fix <path> --migrate unsized` (decision 49 S1f; VERDICTS-40 §5,
# VERDICTS-43 §4, VERDICTS-55) writes every bare `Dyn(...)` type spelling as
# `Box(Dyn(...))`, `*Dyn(...)`/`?*Dyn(...)`, an alias body and a
# `size_of`/`Type.eq` argument included, and leaves `Box`/`Rc`/`&`/`&mut`'s
# argument and `upcast`'s target alone. It lists `Rc(Dyn(T)).new(...)`,
# `impl(Dyn(...), ...)` and a spelling over several lines (multi.yo). The
# evaluation writes `dyn(v)` as `dyn(Box.new(v))` and `dyn(Rc.new(v))` into
# an owning Dyn as `dyn(Box.new(v))`, and lists `dyn(p)` over a raw pointer
# and an owned `downcast`. The second run rewrites nothing, and both files
# still type-check (check only: `dyn(p)` over a raw pointer emits invalid C,
# issues/2026-11-11-dyn-over-a-raw-pointer-emits-invalid-c.md).
fix . --migrate unsized
fix . --migrate unsized
check main.yo
check multi.yo
